<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Max Mallowan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Max Mallowan was a British archaeologist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>. He excavated Chagar Bazar and Tell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brak">Brak</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the 1930s and later led major excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, recovering many of its famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a>. He was the second husband of the novelist Agatha Christie, who accompanied him on his digs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/max-mallowan.jpg" alt="Max Mallowan" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agatha_Christie_and_Max_Mallowan_in_1950.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
